title: Watcher
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: overview
aliases: watcher flush
summary: The watcher follows filesystem events live, buffers them in memory, and records them in batches once the disk has been quiet for two seconds.

The watcher records what happens to tracked files while the daemon runs: a file
created, changed, moved, deleted. Events go through one pipeline whatever produces them:

# a ''source'' delivers them — inotify, one watch per directory (the ''budget'' regime), or a fanotify broker covering whole filesystems (the ''coverage'' regime) ([[Watch sources and regimes]]);
# the ''ingest'' thread counts them ([[Watch activity]]), drops those on ineligible paths ([[Eligibility]]) and buffers the rest, in memory;
# once the filesystem has been quiet for two seconds the ''executor'' flushes the buffer: it compacts it, groups it by kind, and applies each group as one revision ([[Event batching]], [[Event semantics]]).

Only the watcher (and a forced write) sets `mfr_path` to `nothing`: a file seen deleted.
Reconcile never does; it leaves a stale path in place ([[Reconcile]]).

What the watcher cannot see, it cannot record: a daemon that is down, a broker that
disconnected, a queue that overflowed — each such gap is announced, and closed by a
reconcile you run. Nothing is inferred from a gap, and no reconcile is
started on its own.

